C = [
("h1", "10. 1 Timothy 3:16 in the House of God"),
("p", "Claim: 1 Timothy 3:16 is a short confession set in a letter about the order of a church. It names six things in six short lines, and the first is that God was manifest in the flesh. The volume reads the verse as the King James Version has it, reports the question about the Greek fairly, and rests nothing on this verse alone."),
("sub", "10.1 The setting"),
("p", "Paul writes to Timothy that he may know “how thou oughtest to behave thyself in the house of God, which is the church of the living God, the pillar and ground of the truth” (1 Timothy 3:15). The letter is about conduct in the church, and the verse that follows names what the church holds up: “And without controversy great is the mystery of godliness: God was manifest in the flesh, justified in the Spirit, seen of angels, preached unto the Gentiles, believed on in the world, received up into glory” (1 Timothy 3:16)."),
("p", "Thomas Constable remarks that “Paul evidently was quoting a fragment of a hymn, or a statement of the apostolic church.” The volume reports the remark. The letter does not say that Paul is quoting, and the volume reads the lines as the letter has them, as lines Paul affirms."),
("sub", "10.2 The six lines"),
("ctbl", "What 1 Timothy 3:16 says and what it leaves unsaid.", [
    ("The line", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("“God was manifest in the flesh”", "God was made manifest in the flesh", "How God can be manifest in the flesh; the line states the fact"),
    ("“justified in the Spirit”", "He was justified in the Spirit", "What the justifying was and when; the line states it"),
    ("“seen of angels”", "He was seen of angels", "Which angels, and when"),
    ("“preached unto the Gentiles”", "He was preached to the Gentiles", "How the preaching of him relates to the Gentiles’ standing; the line names it"),
    ("“believed on in the world”", "He was believed on in the world", "By whom and how many"),
    ("“received up into glory”", "He was received up into glory", "The manner and the place; Philippians 3:21 speaks of his glorious body"),
  ], [0.28, 0.36, 0.36]),
("sub", "10.3 The mystery of godliness"),
("p", "Paul calls the whole “the mystery of godliness.” The word is the one the volume on the Mystery Program treats in the letters to the Colossians and the Ephesians. The volume does not say that the word has the same sense here, and it does not argue the point. It reports that Paul uses the word of the confession, and that “great” is the adjective he gives it."),
("sub", "10.4 The question about the Greek"),
("p", "The King James Version reads “God was manifest in the flesh.” Some Greek manuscripts have a relative pronoun at the head of the line, and the line then reads //who was manifest in the flesh,// and many modern translations follow that reading. Constable, whose notes follow a modern translation, glosses the line “God revealed Jesus Christ in the flesh.” The volume reads the King James text, and notes that on either reading the six lines that follow are said of one person, who was manifest in the flesh, justified in the Spirit and received up into glory. The thesis does not rest on the first word of this verse. The sayings of Philippians, Colossians and Titus stand without it."),
("sub", "10.5 The same letter at its close"),
("p", "First Timothy ends with a charge that has the same shape. “I give thee charge in the sight of God, who quickeneth all things, and before Christ Jesus, who before Pontius Pilate witnessed a good confession; That thou keep this commandment without spot, unrebukeable, until the appearing of our Lord Jesus Christ” (1 Timothy 6:13, 14). The next verses speak of one “who only hath immortality, dwelling in the light which no man can approach unto; whom no man hath seen, nor can see” (1 Timothy 6:16). The verses do not say which person is meant by the one who only hath immortality, and the volume does not say. It records that the same letter speaks of the man who stood before Pontius Pilate and of the invisible one in the same paragraph, as it does in 3:16 and 2:5. The letter is not embarrassed by the pairing, and it does not stop to explain it."),
("sub", "10.6 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "The six lines are set in a letter about the church’s order, as what the church holds up.",
    "The first line, in the King James Version, says that God was manifest in the flesh.",
    "The Greek has a variant in the first word, and the volume says so and reads the verse as the King James Version has it.",
    "The lines do not explain how God was manifest in the flesh, and the volume does not.",
  ]),
("p", "The verse speaks of one who was manifest in the flesh and received up into glory. The next chapter takes the sentence in which Paul calls the one who is to appear “the great God and our Saviour.”"),
]
